1
00:00:24,668 --> 00:00:28,329
[SPEAKER_00]: We were discussing the other day about the middle way.

2
00:00:29,270 --> 00:00:34,191
[SPEAKER_00]: It was between asceticism, you know, the two extremes.

3
00:00:36,872 --> 00:00:45,955
[SPEAKER_00]: But the middle way is the enduring of thoughts.

4
00:00:46,075 --> 00:00:50,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yeah, that's certainly the way to cultivate the middle way.

5
00:00:51,878 --> 00:00:53,078
[SPEAKER_00]: The way to cultivate, yeah.

6
00:00:53,778 --> 00:01:07,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the sensuality, like, from the context of thoughts, from the point of view of thoughts and mental images that appear in people's minds, sensuality would be acting out of thoughts, immediately.

7
00:01:08,129 --> 00:01:11,131
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have a thought towards something, you just act out of it.

8
00:01:11,392 --> 00:01:16,736
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no reflection, there is no sense restraint, there is no consideration, more or less.

9
00:01:17,637 --> 00:01:21,720
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to... that kind of... that's pretty much what defines sensuality.

10
00:01:22,657 --> 00:01:28,722
[SPEAKER_01]: And as the Buddha said, it's the man's thought, lust and desire in his thoughts, that makes his centrality be there.

11
00:01:29,322 --> 00:01:30,903
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the beautiful things in the world.

12
00:01:32,224 --> 00:01:45,474
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the nature of thought is central thought, but it's also... it's paired with that just immediate acceptance of it, immediate delight in it, immediate welcoming of it, which means immediate acting towards whatever you're thinking of.

13
00:01:47,916 --> 00:01:49,157
[SPEAKER_01]: And the opposite way would be

14
00:01:49,778 --> 00:01:51,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, say, you see sensuality is a problem.

15
00:01:51,959 --> 00:01:52,719
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do you do then?

16
00:01:53,019 --> 00:01:54,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you try to deny the thoughts.

17
00:01:56,660 --> 00:02:02,201
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see that, yes, when these thoughts of sensual desires arise in me, I act out of it.

18
00:02:02,221 --> 00:02:06,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what if I were to not have those thoughts?

19
00:02:06,062 --> 00:02:08,743
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you want to prevent them from arising.

20
00:02:08,783 --> 00:02:15,305
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's now more aligned with that self-mortification, the ignoble way of just self-torture.

21
00:02:16,297 --> 00:02:18,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it can... it cannot bear the result you want.

22
00:02:18,858 --> 00:02:19,718
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's ignoble.

23
00:02:20,118 --> 00:02:22,178
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it does not... it's not wholesome.

24
00:02:23,039 --> 00:02:32,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Not that trying to resist thoughts is bad, but, see, denying thoughts requires you to resist thoughts, but it takes it even further now.

25
00:02:32,161 --> 00:02:33,721
[SPEAKER_01]: It overshoots in the other way.

26
00:02:34,621 --> 00:02:41,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Because one thing is you resisting the thoughts, means exactly that, allowing them to endure, but resisting you acting out of it.

27
00:02:42,243 --> 00:02:45,744
[SPEAKER_01]: And another thing is now to... oh, I mustn't have these thoughts to begin with.

28
00:02:46,526 --> 00:02:47,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, that's you overreaching.

29
00:02:48,187 --> 00:02:49,748
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not up to you.

30
00:02:49,788 --> 00:02:54,231
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why either giving in to those thoughts or trying to deny them, you're equally ignorant.

31
00:02:54,291 --> 00:02:56,393
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either bound with sensuality or self-mortification.

32
00:02:56,413 --> 00:02:59,975
[SPEAKER_01]: You're bound with the same domain of bait, as the Buddha described.

33
00:02:59,995 --> 00:03:01,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Bait that Mahārāja lays down for you.

34
00:03:03,218 --> 00:03:06,220
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you then not go into either of these extremes?

35
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:07,821
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, by the way, it's not really...

36
00:03:07,841 --> 00:03:08,081
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean...

37
00:03:09,428 --> 00:03:13,210
[SPEAKER_01]: It can result in extremes, centrality and self-mortification.

38
00:03:13,631 --> 00:03:16,192
[SPEAKER_01]: But in itself, it's relatively subtle in the beginning.

39
00:03:16,312 --> 00:03:20,215
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like... You denying thoughts, it's not exactly extreme, isn't it?

40
00:03:20,835 --> 00:03:23,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it already partakes in the way of extreme self-mortification.

41
00:03:23,877 --> 00:03:24,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Shares the same nature.

42
00:03:25,418 --> 00:03:26,319
[SPEAKER_01]: It's rooted in that.

43
00:03:27,119 --> 00:03:29,561
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all the acts of self-mortification, they would come

44
00:03:31,027 --> 00:03:37,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Later on, in someone's practice of such kind, would be all sharing the same principle in common.

45
00:03:37,791 --> 00:03:39,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Denial of the arisen thought.

46
00:03:39,952 --> 00:03:40,733
[SPEAKER_01]: I mustn't have it.

47
00:03:42,514 --> 00:03:45,356
[SPEAKER_01]: And even the Buddha was the same initially, when he was trying to find a way out.

48
00:03:46,436 --> 00:03:47,837
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's no end to that.

49
00:03:47,857 --> 00:03:48,878
[SPEAKER_01]: You can always get more.

50
00:03:49,058 --> 00:03:51,119
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can always do more and do more.

51
00:03:51,139 --> 00:03:53,641
[SPEAKER_01]: To the point when the Buddha said, well, what if I were to stop breathing?

52
00:03:54,640 --> 00:04:01,426
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, I will kill myself and I will not... so to not have thoughts, so to not have unwholesome states arise.

53
00:04:02,406 --> 00:04:06,470
[SPEAKER_01]: But then he goes, well, actually, that's... that's not going to help, that's not going to work.

54
00:04:09,177 --> 00:04:16,788
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the reason I'm saying this is because people kind of tend to dismiss, oh yeah, self-mortification, asceticism, or extreme sensuality.

55
00:04:16,828 --> 00:04:17,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are extremes.

56
00:04:18,270 --> 00:04:20,513
[SPEAKER_01]: As long as I don't commit to those extremes, I'm in the middle.

57
00:04:20,593 --> 00:04:20,733
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

58
00:04:21,234 --> 00:04:25,060
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're already either accepting your thoughts or denying them, you're not in the middle.

59
00:04:26,966 --> 00:04:35,348
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the middle way is the way that basically can discern the arisen thought, not act out of it and not try to get rid of it.

60
00:04:35,908 --> 00:04:47,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, the mind already needs to have been developed sufficiently to be able to allow the thought to endure without jumping into the action on account of it.

61
00:04:47,050 --> 00:04:56,372
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sensory strain needs to be developed beforehand, virtue needs to be established, because that will prevent you, that will give you a certain space to not just automatically act out of it.

62
00:04:58,693 --> 00:05:00,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're not denying those thoughts.

63
00:05:00,815 --> 00:05:02,837
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's what I mean.

64
00:05:02,857 --> 00:05:04,018
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a natural progression.

65
00:05:04,078 --> 00:05:08,281
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you see sensuality as a problem, you want to deal with it, so what you do, you deny the whole thing.

66
00:05:08,982 --> 00:05:10,603
[SPEAKER_01]: But you deny things you shouldn't be denying.

67
00:05:10,703 --> 00:05:13,846
[SPEAKER_01]: And by denying them, you're baited, you're hooked to that bait again.

68
00:05:14,427 --> 00:05:16,989
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the deer herd simile in the Majjhima Nikaya.

69
00:05:17,549 --> 00:05:20,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, look, they got caught by engaging in sensuality.

70
00:05:20,412 --> 00:05:23,454
[SPEAKER_01]: What if we stop and we run on top of a mountain and we don't eat, don't drink?

71
00:05:23,815 --> 00:05:24,996
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you can't sustain that,

72
00:05:28,312 --> 00:05:30,814
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the third one is the one that finds the middle way.

73
00:05:31,194 --> 00:05:33,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, what if we don't eat around the bait?

74
00:05:33,376 --> 00:05:34,116
[SPEAKER_01]: We don't eat the bait.

75
00:05:35,017 --> 00:05:43,523
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we don't do that which baits us into dharmāra's domain, but we don't shun it either, because that would all equally make us visible to the hunter.

76
00:05:44,724 --> 00:05:55,592
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the beginning of that discernment, is being able to allow thoughts to endure without welcoming them, because thoughts arise on their own.

77
00:05:55,612 --> 00:05:56,393
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole point.

78
00:05:57,264 --> 00:06:03,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you thinking you can deny them, already implies wrong view, whereby you think you are in charge of those thoughts.

79
00:06:03,214 --> 00:06:04,857
[SPEAKER_01]: You're responsible for their arising.

80
00:06:04,957 --> 00:06:05,097
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

81
00:06:05,578 --> 00:06:07,181
[SPEAKER_01]: You're responsible for their acceptance.

82
00:06:07,621 --> 00:06:09,364
[SPEAKER_01]: You're responsible for delighting in them.

83
00:06:09,805 --> 00:06:11,488
[SPEAKER_01]: You're responsible for acting out of them.

84
00:06:13,478 --> 00:06:16,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't do these things and thoughts are still there, that's not on you.

85
00:06:17,059 --> 00:06:24,140
[SPEAKER_01]: But the reason why people then still want to get rid of those thoughts, is because it's unpleasant to allow thoughts to endure without acting out of it.

86
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:28,661
[SPEAKER_01]: It's easier to act towards sensuality and it's easier to act towards denial.

87
00:06:29,261 --> 00:06:32,722
[SPEAKER_01]: The hardest thing is to allow it to endure and not act out of it.

88
00:06:33,242 --> 00:06:34,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it can endure for days.

89
00:06:37,163 --> 00:06:38,803
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not acting out of it and it's there.

90
00:06:38,823 --> 00:06:42,744
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole pressure, emotional charge, it's all there, but you're not acting out of it.

91
00:06:43,488 --> 00:06:52,410
[SPEAKER_01]: It's easier to just get angry, or get lustful, just say yes, or say no, or act, express yourself quickly, deal with it, whatever it is.

92
00:06:52,590 --> 00:06:53,092
[SPEAKER_01]: It's easier.

93
00:06:54,838 --> 00:07:00,483
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, the only reason you're doing it, is because the endurance of the thought is first, and it's still there.

94
00:07:00,883 --> 00:07:08,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, doesn't matter how much you give in, try to deny it, you always do it on account of that which you're not in control of, that bothers you, which is endurance of the thought.

95
00:07:08,929 --> 00:07:10,050
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it's futile.

96
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:16,135
[SPEAKER_01]: All of your efforts to act... they come out of it, as a reaction to it, are futile.

97
00:07:16,175 --> 00:07:23,481
[SPEAKER_01]: If you really want to deal with the painfulness of enduring thought, where you're not acting out of it, well, you need to allow it to endure and then understand it.

98
00:07:25,780 --> 00:07:27,541
[SPEAKER_00]: See it clearly, understand it.

99
00:07:27,741 --> 00:07:37,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's why the way I said, there is no wisdom arising on account of sensual behavior, and there will be no wisdom arising on account of that self-mortification, asceticism behavior of just saying no to everything.

100
00:07:37,386 --> 00:07:38,467
[SPEAKER_01]: There will be no wisdom.

101
00:07:38,487 --> 00:07:39,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Pushing away or denying.

102
00:07:39,448 --> 00:07:42,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because you just lock into a direction, and that's your principle of action.

103
00:07:43,450 --> 00:07:46,852
[SPEAKER_01]: If it arises, I just say yes, yes, yes, or now you go the other way.

104
00:07:47,152 --> 00:07:50,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever arises, I just say no, no, no, no, no.

105
00:07:51,094 --> 00:07:59,179
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you conflate that with the idea of purity and then you feel even extra superior on account of your great determination that you eat once a month or something.

106
00:07:59,579 --> 00:08:01,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody can do that about you.

107
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:02,040
[SPEAKER_01]: You're so special.

108
00:08:03,041 --> 00:08:11,806
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize even the unwholesome states that you wanted to abandon, the conceit, ego, pride, get amplified and you haven't actually abandoned it.

109
00:08:18,329 --> 00:08:19,690
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the endurance of the thought.

110
00:08:20,766 --> 00:08:26,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever thought... whatever the thought is, whatever the state of mind is, you have to allow it to endure.

111
00:08:28,493 --> 00:08:31,016
[SPEAKER_00]: And that means not act out of it and not try to get rid of it.

112
00:08:31,036 --> 00:08:32,537
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean maintaining.

113
00:08:33,218 --> 00:08:33,438
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

114
00:08:33,458 --> 00:08:34,399
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to keep it in.

115
00:08:34,799 --> 00:08:34,940
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

116
00:08:34,960 --> 00:08:37,342
[SPEAKER_01]: That's again... that's not... it's not up to you.

117
00:08:37,362 --> 00:08:38,023
[SPEAKER_01]: Just ask yourself.

118
00:08:38,323 --> 00:08:40,405
[SPEAKER_01]: The state of mind you have now, whatever mood you're in,

119
00:08:42,332 --> 00:08:43,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you create it?

120
00:08:43,413 --> 00:08:47,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have a... did you, like, press an internal button that produced it?

121
00:08:48,137 --> 00:08:48,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Not at all.

122
00:08:48,817 --> 00:08:50,879
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's what... that's kind of what you would think.

123
00:08:51,039 --> 00:08:52,420
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the kind of... That's the feeling.

124
00:08:52,501 --> 00:08:53,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the implication.

125
00:08:53,702 --> 00:08:56,284
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the people... the view behind it's kind of, oh, this is mine.

126
00:08:56,604 --> 00:08:56,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

127
00:08:57,204 --> 00:09:00,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is exactly why it's like, oh, this is mine, so I engage with it.

128
00:09:00,807 --> 00:09:03,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Or when you start seeing problem with that, this is mine, so I will deny it.

129
00:09:04,851 --> 00:09:06,352
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact is that that's still there.

130
00:09:06,372 --> 00:09:08,394
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize, oh, I can't even deny this.

131
00:09:09,673 --> 00:09:13,876
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the problem is not in those mental states or thoughts arising.

132
00:09:14,557 --> 00:09:18,400
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is in you acting out of it, either towards or against.

133
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:21,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you are affirming their grip over you.

134
00:09:22,743 --> 00:09:25,345
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're trying to wiggle out of it.

135
00:09:30,448 --> 00:09:32,270
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that would be the beginning of the Middle Way.

136
00:09:33,030 --> 00:09:34,572
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly like what...

137
00:09:35,444 --> 00:09:39,507
[SPEAKER_01]: In the suttas, grasping the signs of your mind, cittanimitta, what it is.

138
00:09:40,027 --> 00:09:47,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Grasping the signs of your mind for what it is, the mind, for its nature, the way it has arisen, the way it endures.

139
00:09:47,752 --> 00:09:57,518
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason why people cannot grasp the cittanimitta, a prerequisite for the right view, is because they either act or deny against their own mind.

140
00:09:58,198 --> 00:10:01,881
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the mind of sensuality, they're fully projected towards sensual action already.

141
00:10:02,980 --> 00:10:10,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, the mind of sensuality fully projected to a denial of the entire mind, of all... the root of the thoughts, not just this specific thing.

142
00:10:12,123 --> 00:10:17,925
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're either underdoing the restraint or overdoing the restraint, but you're not doing the restraint properly either way.

143
00:10:24,662 --> 00:10:26,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you'll be watching your breath.

144
00:10:27,283 --> 00:10:28,223
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be watching your breath.

145
00:10:28,884 --> 00:10:33,225
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like some people, you know, doing this meditation, just watching their breath.

146
00:10:34,105 --> 00:10:36,006
[SPEAKER_00]: And then a lustful thought comes up.

147
00:10:36,847 --> 00:10:37,047
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

148
00:10:37,867 --> 00:10:38,627
[SPEAKER_00]: I must get rid of this.

149
00:10:39,427 --> 00:10:40,528
[SPEAKER_00]: I must watch my breath.

150
00:10:41,388 --> 00:10:44,409
[SPEAKER_00]: I must watch my breath so that I don't have lustful thoughts.

151
00:10:44,489 --> 00:10:53,713
[SPEAKER_00]: And if I just keep on watching my breath, watching my breath as hard as I can, then, whatever, eight hours a day, I don't have any lustful thoughts.

152
00:10:53,933 --> 00:10:54,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

153
00:10:55,502 --> 00:10:59,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's like the deer herd that ran on top of a mountain.

154
00:10:59,944 --> 00:11:03,726
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not experiencing any temptation from the bait for a period of time.

155
00:11:04,727 --> 00:11:11,450
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're not growing wisdom in regard to overcoming the bait altogether, which means the food will have to run out.

156
00:11:11,570 --> 00:11:14,972
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you can't sustain meditating, breathing eight hours a day, watching.

157
00:11:15,092 --> 00:11:16,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Something will have to pull you one way or the other.

158
00:11:17,413 --> 00:11:20,514
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll get sick, you have to deal with things, and then you're back to where you were before.

159
00:11:20,534 --> 00:11:21,515
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no wisdom there.

160
00:11:22,255 --> 00:11:26,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Creating, like, elevated ground for you to be on, in regard to all these things that were pulling you around.

161
00:11:26,636 --> 00:11:30,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Lustful thought comes, and you don't have time to watch your bait, because there's stuff going on.

162
00:11:30,957 --> 00:11:39,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the circumstances, the suitable environment that was helping you not engage with the bait, is gone.

163
00:11:39,158 --> 00:11:43,139
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, your freedom from lustful thoughts was only circumstantial.

164
00:11:44,039 --> 00:11:46,200
[SPEAKER_01]: And now circumstances have changed, and you're back to it.

165
00:11:47,204 --> 00:11:53,409
[SPEAKER_01]: And by the way, there is no problem if you have lustful thoughts while you're breathing and you choose to attend to the breath.

166
00:11:53,429 --> 00:11:55,110
[SPEAKER_01]: You can do that, because it is a matter of choice.

167
00:11:55,551 --> 00:11:57,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Will I choose to attend this or choose to attend that?

168
00:11:57,913 --> 00:12:03,117
[SPEAKER_01]: But the problem is when people choose to attend breath with the view of denying the thought.

169
00:12:03,758 --> 00:12:09,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, I will attend my breath and allow this thought to endure in the background of my mind, because it has a reason on its own.

170
00:12:09,462 --> 00:12:10,323
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't invite it.

171
00:12:10,843 --> 00:12:12,365
[SPEAKER_01]: I will allow it to endure as long as it wants.

172
00:12:12,445 --> 00:12:13,826
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not give my attention to it.

173
00:12:13,946 --> 00:12:14,927
[SPEAKER_01]: I will attend to my breath.

174
00:12:15,507 --> 00:12:20,195
[SPEAKER_01]: But not, I will attend to my breath, so this thought is gone, I'll go away with it, it doesn't exist.

175
00:12:20,816 --> 00:12:24,102
[SPEAKER_01]: No, now you... ah, so you're actually engaged with it and your breath is actually secondary.

176
00:12:24,543 --> 00:12:26,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Because this is now your concern, that thought.

177
00:12:27,782 --> 00:12:30,904
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like when the Mahārā comes in the circus and tries to tempt.

178
00:12:31,444 --> 00:12:33,366
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha can't chase him away.

179
00:12:33,386 --> 00:12:36,888
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't kick him and tell him, you know, get lost.

180
00:12:36,908 --> 00:12:39,570
[SPEAKER_01]: He can just not provide him a basis to latch on.

181
00:12:40,150 --> 00:12:41,731
[SPEAKER_01]: And then Mahārā leaves you on his own.

182
00:12:41,811 --> 00:12:47,415
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, thoughts of sensuality come and like flies fly around you, but they have nothing to land on.

183
00:12:48,095 --> 00:12:50,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you provide something suitable for them to land on.

184
00:12:52,912 --> 00:13:02,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when even in the satsang says you don't pay attention to these thoughts, you don't do it at the expense of denying their presence, it's because they're present, you don't give them your attention.

185
00:13:03,739 --> 00:13:07,442
[SPEAKER_01]: I wouldn't have to not give you my attention if you are not here.

186
00:13:09,864 --> 00:13:13,246
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if I'm instructed to not give you my attention, means I know you are here.

187
00:13:13,946 --> 00:13:18,790
[SPEAKER_01]: And for the duration of that endurance of your presence here, I will not give you my attention.

188
00:13:19,656 --> 00:13:24,277
[SPEAKER_01]: But not, I will not give you my attention, I will not give you my attention, I hope he disappears.

189
00:13:24,297 --> 00:13:25,517
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's wishful thinking now.

190
00:13:26,137 --> 00:13:29,978
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're actually still denying the presence of that thing that you had nothing to do with.

191
00:13:30,798 --> 00:13:31,838
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how Māra gets you.

192
00:13:32,818 --> 00:13:33,798
[SPEAKER_01]: That's still his domain.

193
00:13:33,978 --> 00:13:35,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Affirming or denying, it's his domain.

194
00:13:37,219 --> 00:13:41,320
[SPEAKER_00]: So you say it's being economists?

195
00:13:41,760 --> 00:13:43,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that will be the outcome of that.

196
00:13:43,440 --> 00:13:45,760
[SPEAKER_01]: If you cultivate this long enough, Māra will leave you alone.

197
00:13:46,561 --> 00:13:48,741
[SPEAKER_01]: The pressure will not be able to overwhelm your mind.

198
00:13:49,562 --> 00:13:53,263
[SPEAKER_01]: But not if you cultivate sense restraint in itself.

199
00:13:54,324 --> 00:13:56,804
[SPEAKER_01]: That's necessary basis for you to see the middle way.

200
00:13:57,805 --> 00:14:07,308
[SPEAKER_01]: For you to stop denying, trying to deny thoughts and states of mind, but allow them to endure and then basically you become stronger than those states of mind, so to speak.

201
00:14:09,809 --> 00:14:11,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Through having them endure and not acting out of it.

202
00:14:12,090 --> 00:14:14,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Not losing your sense restraint and not trying to deny it.

203
00:14:14,931 --> 00:14:16,211
[SPEAKER_01]: So you're cultivating the middle way.

204
00:14:17,211 --> 00:14:18,772
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yes, you will become economist.

205
00:14:20,170 --> 00:14:20,890
[SPEAKER_01]: It's imperturbable.

206
00:14:22,211 --> 00:14:25,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what you've been cultivating.

207
00:14:25,433 --> 00:14:29,256
[SPEAKER_00]: But those states will dissipate, so to speak.

208
00:14:29,276 --> 00:14:31,437
[SPEAKER_00]: There will be less pressure.

209
00:14:31,578 --> 00:14:35,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, but that's a side effect, so to speak.

210
00:14:36,020 --> 00:14:39,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Your concern is that you not be moved by those states, even if they last forever.

211
00:14:40,884 --> 00:14:42,285
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the attitude that you should have.

212
00:14:43,125 --> 00:14:45,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like I will deny these states forever after.

213
00:14:46,413 --> 00:14:48,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Or I will indulge in these states forever after.

214
00:14:48,114 --> 00:14:48,194
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

215
00:14:48,254 --> 00:14:50,316
[SPEAKER_01]: I would be immovable in regard to these states.

216
00:14:50,356 --> 00:14:57,500
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, be like water, be like fire, be like... be like earth and wind that doesn't get affected by things that sort of come through.

217
00:15:00,682 --> 00:15:08,988
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't practice... like, I mean... if you want to make that an actual factual practice, yeah, you... that's done so on the level of the mental states.

218
00:15:09,428 --> 00:15:10,368
[SPEAKER_01]: What mood am I in?

219
00:15:10,809 --> 00:15:12,590
[SPEAKER_01]: How is that mood affecting my action?

220
00:15:13,858 --> 00:15:14,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I lashing out?

221
00:15:14,878 --> 00:15:15,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I binge eating?

222
00:15:15,899 --> 00:15:17,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I seeking centrality?

223
00:15:17,359 --> 00:15:18,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I irritable?

224
00:15:18,140 --> 00:15:18,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I angry?

225
00:15:18,660 --> 00:15:19,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I expressing it?

226
00:15:19,760 --> 00:15:23,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I being perturbed by these states of mind?

227
00:15:24,082 --> 00:15:25,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Means I don't see the mind for what it is.

228
00:15:25,863 --> 00:15:27,243
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't see its nature for what it is.

229
00:15:27,624 --> 00:15:29,945
[SPEAKER_01]: I am not grasping the sign of the mind correctly.

230
00:15:29,985 --> 00:15:30,985
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind of a phenomenon.

231
00:15:31,005 --> 00:15:34,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, sign of the mind is a sign, oh, there is an enduring state of mind.

232
00:15:35,207 --> 00:15:38,008
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't invite it, but I'm certainly not delighting in it or accepting it.

233
00:15:38,688 --> 00:15:40,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing it for what it is, an enduring phenomenon.

234
00:15:42,192 --> 00:15:43,833
[SPEAKER_01]: And you start seeing that, that's it.

235
00:15:43,933 --> 00:15:45,995
[SPEAKER_01]: You're already understanding mind to a greater degree.

236
00:15:47,176 --> 00:15:51,980
[SPEAKER_00]: But if I start doing this with the idea that... oh, yeah, so that...

237
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:52,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing what?

238
00:15:53,561 --> 00:15:58,746
[SPEAKER_00]: Watching my mind, so that I can get rid of... Well, you will inevitably start doing it in a wrong way as well.

239
00:15:59,987 --> 00:16:07,493
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why the... reading the suttas, the... yeah, you have to start somewhere and then don't take your start at face value.

240
00:16:08,372 --> 00:16:09,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Take it with a pinch of salt, so to speak.

241
00:16:09,774 --> 00:16:13,878
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever you're doing, even if it makes sense, okay, let's see, let's test this.

242
00:16:14,138 --> 00:16:15,920
[SPEAKER_01]: In a sense, let's reflect even more.

243
00:16:16,241 --> 00:16:21,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's see how unperturbed I am when these things arise and so on, or if I think certain thoughts.

244
00:16:21,607 --> 00:16:22,428
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you'll see.

245
00:16:24,110 --> 00:16:25,571
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, you will have to start somehow.

246
00:16:26,152 --> 00:16:28,655
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you do start,

247
00:16:30,518 --> 00:16:36,343
[SPEAKER_01]: You're gonna be starting pretty much, like, on your own terms, as I often say, which is a wrong way to start.

248
00:16:36,423 --> 00:16:37,764
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have no other way than that.

249
00:16:38,245 --> 00:16:39,406
[SPEAKER_01]: So you have to start somehow.

250
00:16:40,347 --> 00:16:48,954
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you recognize that, wait, it's not me contemplating this mind, seeing, oh, my mind is like this, and then I'm sticking to observation of it.

251
00:16:49,315 --> 00:16:52,277
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like just finding the state of mind that's already there.

252
00:16:53,298 --> 00:16:54,359
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already effortless.

253
00:16:54,379 --> 00:16:56,421
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already proper satipaṭṭhāna.

254
00:16:57,748 --> 00:16:59,629
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm mindful of my mind.

255
00:16:59,769 --> 00:17:00,449
[SPEAKER_01]: I am depressed.

256
00:17:00,710 --> 00:17:02,030
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm elated.

257
00:17:02,070 --> 00:17:02,871
[SPEAKER_01]: My mind is lustful.

258
00:17:02,891 --> 00:17:03,851
[SPEAKER_01]: My mind is angry.

259
00:17:04,611 --> 00:17:04,752
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

260
00:17:05,332 --> 00:17:08,213
[SPEAKER_01]: You start like that, but you want to remove yourself from that equation.

261
00:17:08,273 --> 00:17:09,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you doing this observation?

262
00:17:10,014 --> 00:17:16,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, why are you even able to... Oh, my mind is lustful.

263
00:17:16,057 --> 00:17:16,838
[SPEAKER_01]: My mind is angry.

264
00:17:17,678 --> 00:17:18,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you able to do that?

265
00:17:18,719 --> 00:17:20,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind is already that.

266
00:17:21,200 --> 00:17:23,781
[SPEAKER_01]: You find it there, already, enduring as such.

267
00:17:24,161 --> 00:17:26,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so whether I'm doing this or not doesn't matter.

268
00:17:27,708 --> 00:17:36,959
[SPEAKER_01]: What really matters is just not lose that peripheral sight of the state of mind that's enduring, whether I look at it or not look at it, or try to outrun it, or delight in it.

269
00:17:37,059 --> 00:17:37,159
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah!

270
00:17:37,640 --> 00:17:40,683
[SPEAKER_01]: It defines my whole experience as a whole, my whole behavior.

271
00:17:41,685 --> 00:17:46,851
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to see it on that peripheral level, not on the level of I'm attending to my mind, which is kind of impossible.

272
00:17:50,236 --> 00:17:53,418
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you've got a peaceful state of mind, this is the peace...

273
00:17:53,478 --> 00:17:53,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

274
00:17:53,758 --> 00:17:55,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't be negligent in regard to it.

275
00:17:55,880 --> 00:17:56,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't lose it.

276
00:17:56,840 --> 00:17:58,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't distract yourself from it.

277
00:17:58,201 --> 00:17:58,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't ignore it.

278
00:18:00,482 --> 00:18:00,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

279
00:18:00,803 --> 00:18:00,983
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

280
00:18:01,003 --> 00:18:01,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Now it's peaceful.

281
00:18:02,424 --> 00:18:03,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Have I created that peace?

282
00:18:04,345 --> 00:18:05,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Did I create that state of mind?

283
00:18:06,386 --> 00:18:07,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I know where it came from?

284
00:18:07,246 --> 00:18:07,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I know when it's... No.

285
00:18:08,427 --> 00:18:09,408
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm subjected to it.

286
00:18:09,788 --> 00:18:10,689
[SPEAKER_01]: It's agreeable now.

287
00:18:10,709 --> 00:18:10,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

288
00:18:11,009 --> 00:18:14,511
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let me then not delight in agreeability of the state of mind.

289
00:18:15,832 --> 00:18:19,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when that agreeability changes, I will not be affected by it.

290
00:18:20,875 --> 00:18:34,299
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, equally, when the state of mind that endures is disagreeable, you're not delighting in it, which means it's not affecting you as much, which means you don't need to be fretting over and, I must get rid of this, I must deal with this, because it's not affecting you.

291
00:18:35,080 --> 00:18:35,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that was the only problem.

292
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:39,141
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem wasn't that the state of mind arose one way or the other.

293
00:18:39,801 --> 00:18:42,282
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that you were affected by it and you were resisting it.

294
00:18:45,093 --> 00:18:49,436
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not being affected by whatever state of mind, not being affected... Yeah.

295
00:18:50,097 --> 00:18:54,980
[SPEAKER_01]: And then seeing that the state of mind are discerned on a peripheral level, not on the level of your attention.

296
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:56,381
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm looking at my state of mind.

297
00:18:56,922 --> 00:18:59,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind is on the level of sense organs, not sense objects.

298
00:19:00,745 --> 00:19:03,026
[SPEAKER_01]: And what people attend is sense objects.

299
00:19:03,667 --> 00:19:06,088
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm attending to sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, thoughts.

300
00:19:07,690 --> 00:19:10,612
[SPEAKER_01]: But the state of mind is what's peripheral to that.

301
00:19:11,652 --> 00:19:17,195
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have angry thoughts, means you can't have angry thoughts if the mind is not angry.

302
00:19:17,215 --> 00:19:29,443
[SPEAKER_01]: So you recognize, okay, I have angry thoughts, which means the state of mind it endures is a state of anger, which is going to feel ambiguous initially, because people want to have things clear-cut where they can attend to, which is the result of an untrained mind.

303
00:19:30,720 --> 00:19:36,304
[SPEAKER_01]: But training the mind to the sight of the peripheral image of the mind is what developing citta-nimitta is.

304
00:19:36,644 --> 00:19:41,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing the mind for what it is, without over-attending it, without under-attending it and forgetting about it.

305
00:19:41,568 --> 00:19:45,091
[SPEAKER_00]: If you cannot see that, then you cannot endure it.

306
00:19:45,331 --> 00:19:45,611
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

307
00:19:45,631 --> 00:19:48,453
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not seeing that, means you're already not enduring.

308
00:19:48,473 --> 00:19:51,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you are either resisting it or overly welcoming it.

309
00:19:52,657 --> 00:19:56,960
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can't endure those thoughts, you can't endure the mind if you can't see it.

310
00:19:57,584 --> 00:20:00,665
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you have a view that you shouldn't be enduring it, you will never see it.

311
00:20:01,445 --> 00:20:11,449
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, if you have a view that you should be denying those thoughts that represent the state of mind behind them, you will not be able to even endure it, because your view is to not endure it.

312
00:20:11,529 --> 00:20:12,349
[SPEAKER_01]: I must get rid of this.

313
00:20:13,130 --> 00:20:18,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if habitually you just have a state of mind that you immediately just act out of, again, you're not enduring it.

314
00:20:18,652 --> 00:20:20,112
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing it because you can't endure it.

315
00:20:21,092 --> 00:20:24,634
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, the first and foremost, even if you cannot attend

316
00:20:25,668 --> 00:20:37,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Properly, in that peripheral manner, to your state of mind, the only way you would be able to do so, if you start enduring whatever emotional mood you're in, not get out of it, not try to get rid of it.

317
00:20:38,639 --> 00:20:39,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, but sensuality is bad.

318
00:20:39,720 --> 00:20:40,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, it's very bad.

319
00:20:41,161 --> 00:20:44,163
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't act out of it, you don't welcome it, you don't delight in it.

320
00:20:44,544 --> 00:20:49,367
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean you go out and try to remove these thoughts that are there, because that's impossible.

321
00:20:49,467 --> 00:20:50,628
[SPEAKER_01]: That means you're engaging with it.

322
00:20:51,409 --> 00:20:58,074
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to basically be movable in regard to that which moves you, not get out and remove that which moves you.

323
00:20:58,254 --> 00:20:58,995
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

324
00:20:59,675 --> 00:21:05,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Because how can you go out and move that which moves you without being moved, without moving yourself?

325
00:21:05,978 --> 00:21:06,339
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

326
00:21:06,779 --> 00:21:12,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to be movable by removing, making effort to remove that which moves me.

327
00:21:12,862 --> 00:21:14,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then you're not immovable.

328
00:21:14,743 --> 00:21:15,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you've been moved.

329
00:21:15,443 --> 00:21:17,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have to go and move this away.

330
00:21:17,484 --> 00:21:17,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

331
00:21:17,664 --> 00:21:18,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Which you're trying to get rid of.

332
00:21:18,465 --> 00:21:28,210
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to become immovable, imperturbable, as the Buddha would say in the suttas, is to then stop interfering with that which perturbs you and actually stop being perturbed by it, while it's there.

333
00:21:30,800 --> 00:21:35,641
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're perturbed by it, by acting out of it, you're perturbed by it, by trying to get rid of it.

334
00:21:35,681 --> 00:21:40,522
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like when the Buddha said to Angulimala, he says, stop running, stop running.

335
00:21:40,542 --> 00:21:42,062
[SPEAKER_01]: No, he said it to the Buddha, stop running.

336
00:21:42,082 --> 00:21:42,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he said.

337
00:21:42,682 --> 00:21:44,743
[SPEAKER_01]: And I stopped, I stopped moving, yeah.

338
00:21:44,783 --> 00:21:46,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're the one that's running.

339
00:21:46,463 --> 00:21:48,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, you're the one that still moves.

340
00:22:00,607 --> 00:22:02,349
[SPEAKER_01]: So, allowing thoughts, state of mind, to endure.

341
00:22:04,572 --> 00:22:10,119
[SPEAKER_01]: If they're pleasant, endurance will be manifested by you not acting towards that pleasure.

342
00:22:11,320 --> 00:22:18,009
[SPEAKER_01]: If they're unpleasant, the endurance will be manifested by you not acting or trying to get rid of that displeasure.

343
00:22:21,158 --> 00:22:23,820
[SPEAKER_01]: And you see, sense-restraint is already developing that principle.

344
00:22:23,880 --> 00:22:31,165
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you start acting beforehand, being sense-restrained and virtuous, and then you actually get to understand the principle behind it.

345
00:22:31,205 --> 00:22:35,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Which results, by understanding of it, will result in immovability.

346
00:22:36,148 --> 00:22:41,471
[SPEAKER_01]: But that would have not been possible if there was no behavior of sense-restraint being performed beforehand.

347
00:22:41,631 --> 00:22:43,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Wildness, running around.

348
00:22:45,454 --> 00:22:46,835
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what Middle Way is.

349
00:22:50,388 --> 00:22:51,610
[SPEAKER_00]: The immovable.

350
00:22:51,630 --> 00:22:58,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the complete immovability in the face of any phenomenon or state of mind that arises.

351
00:23:02,025 --> 00:23:02,947
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't ask for more.

352
00:23:03,127 --> 00:23:03,748
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need more.

